# Fractions and Decimals: Parts of Wholes, Done Precisely

> Fractions say how many parts a whole is cut into; decimals say the
> same thing in place-value language. Fluency in both — and in moving
> between them — underpins nearly every later topic in mathematics.

## 1. Anatomy of a Fraction

A **fraction** a/b records a division: numerator ÷ denominator. The
**denominator** names the size of the parts; the **numerator** counts
how many are taken.

| Type | Condition | Example | Value |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Proper | numerator < denominator | 3/4 | less than 1 |
| Improper | numerator ≥ denominator | 7/4 | 1 or more |
| Mixed number | whole + proper part | 1¾ = 7/4 | equals the improper form |
| Like fractions | same denominator | 2/9 and 5/9 | add directly |
| Unlike fractions | different denominators | 2/3 and 3/5 | need a common step |

**Equivalent fractions** scale top and bottom alike: 2/3 = 4/6 =
20/30; **simplifying** divides both by the HCF: 36/48 = 3/4 (÷ 12).

## 2. Adding and Subtracting: Common Ground First

Like fractions add straight across. **Unlike fractions** must first be
re-cut to a **common denominator** — best chosen as the LCM of the
denominators.

[example]
Add 3/4 + 5/6.
- LCM(4, 6) = 12, so convert: 3/4 = 9/12 and 5/6 = 10/12.
- Add: 9/12 + 10/12 = 19/12 = 1 7/12.
- Subtraction works the same way: 5/6 − 3/4 = 10/12 − 9/12 = 1/12.
[/example]

[warning]
Never add denominators: 3/4 + 5/6 is not 8/10 — different-sized
pieces must be re-cut to one size before counting them together.
[/warning]

## 3. Multiplying and Dividing

Multiplication needs no common denominator: multiply straight across,
and cancel common factors before multiplying to keep the arithmetic
small.

[example]
Multiply 15/8 × 4/9. Cancel diagonally: 15 with 9 (÷3 gives 5/3) and
4 with 8 (÷4 gives 1/2). Only 5 × 1 over 3 × 2 remains: 5/6.
Divide by flipping: 3/4 ÷ 2/5 = 3/4 × 5/2 = 15/8 = 1 7/8.
[/example]

To **divide**, multiply by the **reciprocal** of the divisor — "invert
and multiply" is the definition, not a trick.

## 4. Comparing Fractions

| Technique | Best when | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Common denominator | any pair, exact | 3/4 vs 5/6 → 9/12 vs 10/12 |
| Cross-multiply | quick pair test | 3×6 = 18 < 4×5 = 20, so 3/4 < 5/6 |
| Benchmark ½ | fast estimating | 5/9 beats ½; 4/9 does not |
| Same numerator | shared tops | 3/7 < 3/5 — more pieces, smaller pieces |

[tip]
With the same numerator, the larger denominator gives the smaller
fraction — 7 pieces are each smaller than 5.
[/tip]

## 5. Fractions, Decimals, and Percentages

Every fraction becomes a decimal by dividing numerator by denominator,
and a percentage by multiplying that decimal by 100.

| Fraction | Decimal | Percent |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1/2 | 0.5 | 50% |
| 1/3 | 0.333… | 33.3% |
| 1/4 | 0.25 | 25% |
| 1/5 | 0.2 | 20% |
| 1/8 | 0.125 | 12.5% |
| 3/8 | 0.375 | 37.5% |
| 1/9 | 0.111… | 11.1% |

Memorising this table converts most exam fractions on sight: 5/8 is
0.625 because 5/8 = 4/8 + 1/8 = 0.5 + 0.125.

## 6. Terminating or Repeating?

A fraction in lowest terms gives a **terminating decimal** exactly
when the denominator's prime factors are only 2s and 5s.

| Fraction | Denominator factors | Decimal |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 7/40 | 2³ × 5 | 0.175 (terminates) |
| 5/12 | 2² × 3 | 0.41666… (repeats) |
| 7/9 | 3² | 0.777… (repeats) |

The reason: 10 = 2 × 5, so only 2s and 5s divide a power of 10
evenly. Any other prime in the denominator forces the long-division
remainders to cycle forever, producing a **recurring decimal**.

## 7. Decimal Operations and Rounding

- Add or subtract: line up the decimal points, then column-work:
  3.45 + 0.8 = 4.25.
- Multiply: ignore the points, multiply, then plant as many decimal
  places as the factors had combined: 0.3 × 0.02 = 0.006 (1 + 2 = 3
  places).
- Divide: shift the divisor's point until it is whole, shift the
  dividend identically, then divide as usual.
- **Powers of ten** move the point: × 10 shifts one place right, ÷ 100
  shifts two places left (2.75 ÷ 100 = 0.0275).

To round, inspect the next digit: 3.4567 to 2 d.p. looks at the 6 (≥ 5),
giving 3.46.

## 8. Recurring Decimals Back to Fractions

[example]
Convert x = 0.777… (7 recurring) to a fraction.
- Let x = 0.777…, so 10x = 7.777…
- Subtract: 10x − x = 7.777… − 0.777… = 7, hence 9x = 7.
- Therefore x = 7/9.

For a two-digit repeat, shift by 100 instead: 100x − x = 23 for
0.232323…, so x = 23/99.
[/example]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Task | Method | Answer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 3/4 + 5/6 | LCM 12 → 9/12 + 10/12 | 19/12 = 1 7/12 |
| 15/8 × 4/9 | cancel 15↔9, 8↔4 | 5/6 |
| 3/4 ÷ 2/5 | multiply by 5/2 | 15/8 = 1 7/8 |
| 0.777… | 9x = 7 | 7/9 |
| Does 7/40 terminate? | 40 = 2³ × 5 only | yes — 0.175 |
| Round 3.4567 to 2 d.p. | third digit 6 ≥ 5 | 3.46 |
